===Etymology 1===
From {{m|en|Quine}}, {{named-after|en|Willard Van Orman Quine|wplink==|nat=the American|occ=logician and philosopher|born=1908|died=2000|nocap=1}}.

Noun {{senseno|en|computing}} (“program that produces its own source code as output”) and verb {{senseno|en|append}} (“to append (a text) to a quotation of itself”) were {{coinage|en|Douglas Hofstadter|nat=the American|occ=cognitive and computer scientist|nocap=1}} (born 1945) in his book ''[[w:Gödel, Escher, Bach|Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid]]'' (1979), referring to Quine’s study of {{w|indirect self-referencing}} and in particular [[w:Quine's paradox|Quine’s paradox]], the following statement that produces a paradox: “‘Yields falsehood when preceded by its quotation’ yields falsehood when preceded by its quotation.”

Verb {{senseno|en|philosophy}} (“to deny the importance or significance of (something obviously real or important)”) was independently {{coinage|en|Daniel Dennett|nat=the American|occ=cognitive scientist and philosopher|nocap=1}} (1942–2024) in September 1969 in the original version of his work ''{{w|The Philosophical Lexicon}}'':<ref>{{cite-book|author=w:Daniel Dennett|chapter=Preface to the Eighth Edition|mainauthor={{w|Kathleen Atkins}} [''et al.'']|editor=Daniel Dennett|title=The Philosophical Lexicon|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710214239/https://dl.tufts.edu/downloads/1z40m5090?filename=jw827p68f.pdf|location=Newark, Del.|publisher={{w|American Philosophical Association}}, distributor|edition=8th|year=1987|oclc=24233128|passage=The ''Lexicon'' began one night in September of 1969 when I was writing lecture notes and found myself jotting down as a heading “quining intentions”. I saw fit to compose a definition of the verb. In the morning I was ill prepared to lecture, but handed a list of about a dozen definitions together with the Introduction to my colleagues at Irvine.}}</ref> see the 1987 quotation.

